prompt:
Draw a clear night-time picture of a small ship at sea, seen from another boat close by, in plain side view. Draw the ship's bow (her pointed front end) at the LEFT of the picture and her stern at the RIGHT, with her mast standing up in the middle.
A ship's navigation lamps are DIRECTIONAL: each lamp shines only over its own arc around the ship, and from outside that arc you cannot see it at all. The arcs are:
- white masthead lamp, high on the mast: an arc of 225 degrees, reaching from dead ahead round to 112.5 degrees on each side;
- green starboard sidelight, low near the bow: an arc of 112.5 degrees, reaching from dead ahead round to 112.5 degrees on the STARBOARD (right-hand) side of the ship only;
- red port sidelight, low near the bow: an arc of 112.5 degrees, reaching from dead ahead round to 112.5 degrees on the PORT (left-hand) side of the ship only;
- white sternlight, low at the stern: an arc of 135 degrees, reaching from dead astern round to 67.5 degrees on each side.
YOUR VIEWPOINT: your boat lies 100 degrees round from her bow, on her STARBOARD side, so you are looking at her starboard side.
Work out which of those four lamps have your viewpoint inside their arc, and draw ONLY those. Leave every other lamp dark and undrawn. Draw each lit lamp as a big, clearly coloured glowing disc on the ship, large enough that its colour is unmistakable. Keep the rest of the night dark: no cabin lights, no deck floodlights, no harbour lights, no other coloured light anywhere in the picture, and no text, labels or captions.
